// S~S  SimpleSal signal starts  S~S

   [Time As A Signal.note]              search in ssTEA source files for a reference to this file.
//
   Limits on information in this note:

      The core concepts of ssTEA are complex and so cannot be described well by an Ascii text file.

   The "Time Math" note and the "Time As An ..." notes are focused on extended comments about the
   software implementation of the complex ideas; references include the file and a search phrase.

   -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
   .\SimpleSal\ssTEA\ssTEA_data_dcl.h                   A Time Variable Contains Time Unit Variables
   -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

   Seconds, hours, minutes, and days are used by humans; those measurements can be made by a human.
   Concepts like months and years and seasons are culture-based measurements; ssTEA ignores them.

   Starting from the shortest measurement of Time possible, the planck, and then moving to the
   measurements of nanoseconds, microseconds, and milliseconds takes the reader to a strange world.
   The first task is to learn "ns" and "nanos", "us" and "micros", and "ms" and "millis".  Required.

   The plancks value is not included in ssTEA's awareness of Time.  ssTEA uses the plancks field.

   A planck count this minute is "perceived" just as a count of the number of seconds this minute is
   perceived; ssTEA perceives a planck count as a psuedo-random number.  The number's value is
   created when a math operation creates a Time result.  For example, a Time value is set to zero.
   This aids in debugging and/or understanding; you can see visually, no ssTEA operation changed it.

   The nanosecond count (within one microsecond) may be OPT_IN selected to be included in Time Math.
   This variable creates a method of prioritizing multiple events that occur at the same microsecond.

   The microsecond count (within one millisecond) is the smallest Time Unit where the measurement
   of the count (of microseconds) is trustworthy and represents reality because it is based on a
   hardware counter accessible by the processor.  ssTEA reads this counter and does simple math.

   Scheduling 3 events at the same microsecond with incrementing nanosecond values will cause them
   to be granted agency in incrementing Time order, which is a simple form of relative priorities.
   The higher the number in the nanos, the lower priority is, because it will occur later in Time.

   -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
   -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
   .\SimpleSal\ssTEA\ssT_time_def.h                            Time knowledge from perceived signals
   -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
   counters: 32- bit values expressing a number of milliseconds or a number of microseconds.
   32-bits microseconds allows for a 4,294-second wrap, which is around 71 mins (if incremented once
   each microsecond). TimeCurr advances each time ssTEA_Agency_Root is called, by Micros since Last.
   -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
   (CurrFinish - CurrStart) is Time spent in Loop doing Agencying and App software.
   (CurrStart - LastFinish) is Time spent in the Host OS.
   -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
   -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
   .\SimpleSal\ssTEA\ssT_time_def.h                               The source of knowledge about Time
   -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

   "loop" is called continuously as the idle task in an Arduino App; that is the Arduino Host OS.
   On most mesas, knowledge of elapsed time comes directly from mesa and processor hardware,
   so the basis for "right now" throughout the application is a function of chip counter values.
   Everything that happens in this iteration of loop happens "at this moment of Time".  Right Now.

   SimpleSal Timekeeping is implemented through counter-based and time-based FSMs. A counter-based
   Time FSM is within the loop function; the time-based Time FSMs occur within the ssTEA Time code.

   -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
//




//
   ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
   in electronics, increased heat slows the flow of electrons needed to deliver clocks as signals.
   As the flow goes down, Time slows in the hardware itself; then, software compares the calculated
   speed of power-subtree-with-no-load-ever to the calculated speed of the loaded and heated subtree.
   The heat level is implied when a set of instructions requiring N clocks in heated electronics
   at the currently configured frequency take much longer than expected by the unload subtree code.
   ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
   Time of execution in a processor is "(clock frequency) * (clocks)".  The clock tree drives the
   processor at a frequency, creating a clock the processor uses to perform a sequence of logic.
   This nested clock within a clock propagates at all levels within the clock frequency subtree.
   Interaction between clock trees and frequencies is a fundamental computer design concept.
   "move reg to reg" is 1 clock, while an instruction requring many clocks is doing a complex
   mesa service (floating math) or is managing the interface to another clock domain.
   at the end of the N clocks, the result promised by the processor data sheet while be true.
   ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
   -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
   Consider how to accumulate a measure of Time or compute a difference between two Times,
   in order to allocate the consumption of Time to the right participants.  The form of the Time
   values is a 32-bit counter of the number of microseconds since the processor began to process.

   In this description, each vertical bar ('|')
    represents a moment in Time; each word represents a period of Time between moments in Time.
    Another word for moment is boundary; each word is bounded by a moment at each end of the period.

   The '|' moment-in-Time indicator does not consume any Time; it is a boundary marker between Times.

  |Host1|loopEntry1|ssEntry1|ssExit1|App1|loopExit1|HostN|loopEntryN|ssEntryN|ssExitN|AppN|loopExitN|

   If the microsecond counter is sampled at each of the '|' boundaries, all of Time is accounted for.

   Between ssEntry1's exit boundary and
           ssExit1's entry boundary there exists a
           computable microsecond count    (Exit - Entry) = # of microseconds executing software

//

   -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
   Nomenclature:

   -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
   ssTEA defines a way to describe a moment in Time, a Time variable.  Comparing two Time variables,
   software can decide "did this moment in Time occur before or after another moment in Time?", or,
   "did two Time Events occur at the same moment in Time?".  This is the same operation as comparing
   two integer variables in software: "if (i < 10)".  The comparison is made in the processor; while
   using invisible register operations, the complete logic to compare the two values is on the page.
   -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
   Time variables simply extend the set of values that participate in the comparison, in an order.
   The complete process to compare two Time values considers the values and the order: an algorithm.
   Comparing two Time variables is implemented using logic or math: "if (LessThan(TimeA, TimeB))".
   -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
   Using logic or math, as described for integer comparisons above, the comparison is extended by a
   structure with multiple values in a hierarchy and an algorithm that understands the structure.

   Time Math using Logic Makes Time Into An Arrow

   If the number of days in TimeA is more than in TimeB, TimeA is after TimeB, algorithm completed.

   "Time is an Arrow" in this usage model because the only math supported is adding positive Times.
   This is because the software for "carry the 1" AND the data structure logic is easy to implement.
   -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
   Time Math using Logic Makes Time Into An FSM

   The number of days in TimeA is subtracted from the number of days in TimeB:
    if the result is a positive integer, TimeA is after TimeB, algorithm completed
    if the result is a negative integer, TimeA is before TimeB, algorithm completed
    if the result is zero, TimeA and TimeB are the same value so far, continue to other Time units

   "Time is an FSM" becomes possible when the Time Math is based on math; the FSM can compute the
   result of the addition or subtraction of positive or negative integers; see ssTEA v2.0 for this.
   -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
   The value to the ability to subtract Times might be in support of a product feature: if the host
   instructed me to respond 10 seconds from now, and it takes me 2 seconds to prepare the response,
   at what Time should I start working on the response to have it freshly ready, just in time?.
   Work out the math for the response Time assuming it is currently hour 1, minute 0, second 55.
   Work out the math for the response Time assuming it is currently hour 1, minute 59, second 1.
   -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------


//
   A Time value is an ordered sequence of related Time Unit (TU) values.  Each Time Unit is also a
   member of a pairing with the Time Unit it subdivides, e.g., minutes subdivide hours.  Visually,
   which means "under the automagic processes the mind uses to derive a meaning of a word based on
   the context it was used in", some TU expressions are: "days" or "milliseconds" or "+2" or "-1".
   Two related TU expressions separated by a period describe a Time Unit Pair (TUpair).
   The relationship is defined by human usage, such as days.minutes or seconds.milliseconds.
   This is unrelated to the memory format of the numbers, which could be anything (array, vars).
   The pairing exists in the human expressions, then the human mind, and then code the mind writes.
   -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
   -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
   Each TU of a Time is in a value state:
      The state of the value, e.g., for value N: positive ("+N"), negative ("-N"), or zero ("~0").
      Using this definition "positive" means "a signed integer greater than 0".  0 is not positive.
      Similarly, 0 is not negative, which means "a signed integer value less than 0".
//
   The Time itself is in a value state:  positive, negative, or zero.
      Th Time value state depends on the TU value states: either positive (all TUs positive or zero),
      negative (all TUs are negative or zero), or zero (all TUs are zero).  Time values summarize
      these distinctions only in software as booleans (e.g., Time fields IsPositive and IsZero).
//
   Each pair is a Whole TU value and a Fraction TU value, expressed as "Whole.Fraction" and "W.F".
      The TUpair state only exists within the algorithm that transitions between states in an FSM;
        these states are necessary to properly execute the Whole.Fraction Time math FSM: WF_FSM.
      Each W.F has a Whole value sign state and a fraction value sign state, based on their values.
   -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
//
   Time math IS LIKE dollar math used every day to decide if we can afford 4 dollars and 85 cents.
   Add a tax of 0 dollars and 30 cents (or 0.30 dollars) to that and you need a total of $5.15.
   The Fraction portion of a dollars.cents number is the cents, and ranges from 0 to 99.
      The reason the Fraction ranges from 0 to 99 is that cents subdivide dollars completely.
   The Whole portion of a dollars.cents number is dollars, and ranges from 0 to some finite maximum.
//
   -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
   Time math IS LIKE dollar math: a Time variable has TUpairs: days.hours, hours.minutes, etc.,
    such as "+1.+3" (e.g., right now, it is 1 minute and 3 seconds "past zero", or, "after zero").
   A Whole.Fraction value can be added to or subtracted from a Whole.Fraction value.  Using "+1.+3",
   and "~0.+4" (e.g., 4 seconds from now), we can express "it will happen in +1.+7 minutes.seconds".
//
   Time math IS NOT LIKE dollar math: the Dollars Number does not subdivide a higher level Number,
   positive or negative.  In Dollar math, a usual question is whether the Whole is negative or zero.
//
   -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
   The value zero is given a sign of '~' for several reasons, primarily to do with number notation.
   The human understanding of the state machine is aided by the use of zero state with a sign.
   The ssT math operations use the state to take the appropriate variety of actions with the TUs.
   The reasons, in order of VERY important to important, include:
     1) the math FSM requires knowledge of the value of a TU as well as the sign of the TU
     1) it is harder to read things like +0 or -0 and not get overloaded, '~' is weird but new.
     2) a TU with the value 0 adopts or learns its own sign from a value related to it, WHEN USED.
        a) a whole portion of TUpair with a negative fraction and a zero whole is used as "-0.-X".
        b) a TUpair "~0.+3" is used as "+0.+3"
        c) a TUpair "+3.~0" is used as "+3.+0"
        d) a special case TUpair "~0.~0" derives the proper sign from the Time when used
                an example Z=true:  +1 day, +2 hours, +0 minutes, +0 seconds, +1 ms, +2 us, +3ns
                an example Z=false: -1 day, -2 hours, -0 minutes, -0 seconds, -1 ms, -2 us, -3ns
//
   Time Math operations span all of the TUs in a Time variable, one TUpair of related TUs at a time.
   This is a set of data variables needed for an algorithm about days, hours, minutes, seconds, etc.
   -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
   The collection of TUs from the Whole up to the EON is described by the Prefix; the Prefix includes
   the Whole when deciding if a TU needs math, it does not include the Whole during the TUpair math.
   The TUpair FSM algorithm differentiates between "a TUpair Prefix is NonZero" or "not NonZero".
   The NonzeroTUmap is a state indicator of the zero/nonzero state of each TU in the Time value. If
   the state of every TU is zero in the Prefix portion, the computation is complete for this Lvalue.
   -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
   Unlike Dollar number expressions, Time units (e.g., hours or milliseconds) do not all have the
   same number of subunits (e.g., there are 0-59 hours possible in an hour, but there are 0-999
   milliseconds possible in a second).  Each Time unit has to be considered independently, while
   at the same time is must be considered within the hierarchy of Time units and their relationship.
//
     In the number 123, there is: 1 hundred; 2 tens; 3 ones;    Each field ranges from 0-9.
      If we add the number 95 to 123, the result is: 218.       The tens carried into the hundreds.
//
   Time specification include the Time unit of the FIRST value in the expression (not the last).
     In the Time   6.1.23hr   : 6 hours, 1 minute, 23 seconds.   range for these fields: 0-59
     In the Time   2.55.583mn : 2 minutes, 55 seconds, 583 millisconds.  range of millis is 0-999.
//
   The EON unit bounds the math operations; other subunits are coherent with human Time.
   An EON is the largest unit of Time, so if your math operation changes the EON, that is an error.
   ssTEA's principle is to be explicit, so all the values and relationships are stated here.
//
   -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
   Any finite Time representation has an upper limit, because it is finite.  Working on infinite.
   The number range for any TU has no effect on the memory and computational requirements, the
   range is completely based on Time as defined by humans.  The number range of days is arbitrary.
   In geology, the largest unit of time measurement (farthest from the planck) is an "eon".  Humans
   think of it as "the number of days we have been able to count days"; ssTEA uses a 1,000 day max.
   This provides a range of valid math operations (not overflowing or underflowing) of 2.7 years.
   Part of the reason for choosing 1,000 is to prove that the choice is completely arbitrary.
//
   -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

// S~S  SimpleSal signal ends  S~S
